WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.083
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:08.677
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:17.028
Unit eighteen: Water and Conflict. In this unit, you will discuss shared rivers, water rights and cooperation.

4
00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:19.412
water scarcity.

5
00:00:20.112 --> 00:00:23.923
water scarcity means a lack of enough clean water to meet demand.

6
00:00:24.223 --> 00:00:27.488
Water scarcity affects billions of people.

7
00:00:28.188 --> 00:00:31.827
Your turn. Say: water scarcity.

8
00:00:34.527 --> 00:00:35.923
water scarcity.

9
00:00:36.917 --> 00:00:38.359
transboundary river.

10
00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:43.868
transboundary river means a river that flows through more than one country.

11
00:00:44.168 --> 00:00:48.118
The Mekong is a transboundary river shared by six countries.

12
00:00:48.818 --> 00:00:51.970
Your turn. Say: transboundary river.

13
00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.216
transboundary river.

14
00:00:57.217 --> 00:00:58.415
hydropower dam.

15
00:00:59.115 --> 00:01:04.017
hydropower dam means a dam that uses flowing water to produce electricity.

16
00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:07.129
New hydropower dams have changed the river's flow.

17
00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:10.911
Your turn. Say: hydropower dam.

18
00:01:13.611 --> 00:01:15.135
hydropower dam.

19
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.383
water rights.

20
00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:21.615
water rights means legal rights to use water from a source.

21
00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:25.041
Farmers and factories are arguing over water rights.

22
00:01:25.741 --> 00:01:28.370
Your turn. Say: water rights.

23
00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.211
water rights.

24
00:01:33.217 --> 00:01:34.160
aquifer.

25
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.450
aquifer means an underground layer of rock that holds water.

26
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.040
Over-pumping is draining the aquifer.

27
00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.833
Your turn. Say: aquifer.

28
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:48.523
aquifer.

29
00:01:49.517 --> 00:01:51.029
drought resilience.

30
00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:56.352
drought resilience means the ability to cope with long periods without rain.

31
00:01:56.652 --> 00:01:59.569
Rainwater tanks improve drought resilience.

32
00:02:00.269 --> 00:02:03.943
Your turn. Say: drought resilience.

33
00:02:06.643 --> 00:02:07.899
drought resilience.

34
00:02:08.883 --> 00:02:10.302
water diplomacy.

35
00:02:11.002 --> 00:02:15.394
water diplomacy means negotiation between countries about shared water.

36
00:02:15.694 --> 00:02:19.144
Water diplomacy can prevent conflict between neighbours.

37
00:02:19.844 --> 00:02:23.031
Your turn. Say: water diplomacy.

38
00:02:25.731 --> 00:02:27.103
water diplomacy.

39
00:02:28.117 --> 00:02:29.385
sediment flow.

40
00:02:30.085 --> 00:02:34.685
sediment flow means the movement of soil and sand carried by a river.

41
00:02:34.985 --> 00:02:37.391
Dams reduce sediment flow to the delta.

42
00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:41.161
Your turn. Say: sediment flow.

43
00:02:43.861 --> 00:02:45.060
sediment flow.

44
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.340
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.381
Ms. Sarah, why are fishermen on the Tonle Sap worried?

46
00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:57.900
Upstream dams have cut down the natural flood pulse that feeds the lake.

47
00:02:59.217 --> 00:03:02.029
So hydropower dams affect countries downstream?

48
00:03:03.317 --> 00:03:08.906
Absolutely. They hold back water and sediment flow, which the delta relies on.

49
00:03:10.217 --> 00:03:12.611
Can the countries sort this out peacefully?

50
00:03:13.917 --> 00:03:19.448
Through water diplomacy, yes. Nobody can afford to back out of negotiations.

51
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.227
And if droughts get worse?

52
00:03:23.517 --> 00:03:28.268
Then communities must step up drought resilience and protect their aquifers.

53
00:03:29.583 --> 00:03:33.696
Grammar focus: Advanced phrasal verbs.

54
00:03:33.996 --> 00:03:37.807
Advanced phrasal verbs are common in natural speech.

55
00:03:38.107 --> 00:03:43.752
Hold back means restrain, sort out means resolve, and step up means increase.

56
00:03:44.052 --> 00:03:47.978
For academic writing, you can often use the formal equivalent.

57
00:03:48.278 --> 00:03:54.016
At C1, know both the phrasal verb (natural speech) and its formal equivalent (academic writing).

58
00:03:55.017 --> 00:03:58.087
Now read and listen. The Mekong Under Pressure.

59
00:03:58.387 --> 00:04:02.894
The Mekong River flows through six countries before reaching the sea in Vietnam.

60
00:04:04.183 --> 00:04:08.958
Over sixty million people rely on it for food, water and income.

61
00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:16.255
In recent years, dozens of hydropower dams have been built along the river and its tributaries.

62
00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:22.313
These dams produce clean electricity, but they hold back water and sediment.

63
00:04:23.617 --> 00:04:29.331
Fishermen on the Tonle Sap report falling catches as the natural flood pulse weakens.

64
00:04:30.617 --> 00:04:35.774
Meanwhile, droughts and water scarcity are becoming more frequent.

65
00:04:37.083 --> 00:04:43.216
The Mekong River Commission encourages water diplomacy and data sharing between countries.

66
00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:49.941
Whether the river remains a source of life or conflict depends on cooperation.

67
00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:53.879
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:54.179 --> 00:04:57.433
A river that flows through more than one country is a blank.

69
00:04:57.733 --> 00:05:03.766
A, transboundary river. B, aquifer. C, sediment flow.

70
00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:11.085
The answer is A: transboundary river.

71
00:05:12.583 --> 00:05:15.198
We need to blank this problem before the meeting.

72
00:05:15.498 --> 00:05:19.744
A, sort out. B, back out. C, hold back.

73
00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:26.098
The answer is A: sort out.

74
00:05:27.583 --> 00:05:30.512
The government must blank its efforts to save water.

75
00:05:30.812 --> 00:05:35.173
A, cut down. B, step up. C, hold back.

76
00:05:39.183 --> 00:05:41.554
The answer is B: step up.

77
00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.605
An underground layer of rock that holds water is an blank.

78
00:05:46.905 --> 00:05:51.604
A, aquifer. B, hydropower dam. C, water right.

79
00:05:55.617 --> 00:05:58.232
The answer is A: aquifer.

80
00:05:59.717 --> 00:06:03.434
According to the text, how many people rely on the Mekong?

81
00:06:03.734 --> 00:06:09.617
A, six million. B, over sixty million. C, six hundred million.

82
00:06:13.617 --> 00:06:16.394
The answer is B: over sixty million.

83
00:06:17.883 --> 00:06:22.904
Now it's your turn. Part one: How should countries share the Mekong River fairly?

84
00:06:28.204 --> 00:06:34.221
Part two: Use two phrasal verbs, propose a solution, and explain one difficulty.

85
00:06:40.521 --> 00:06:52.786
Countries should step up data sharing and agree on limits before building new dams. The main difficulty is that upstream countries may not want to cut down on hydropower, because it brings income and energy.

86
00:06:53.086 --> 00:06:55.004
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:56.017 --> 00:07:01.107
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced phrasal verbs.

88
00:07:01.407 --> 00:07:05.859
Today's value is love of country. Love and take care of Cambodia.

89
00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:08.846
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:09.146 --> 00:07:12.124
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

